   "content": [
      {
         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><span id=\"htZoomImageMsg\"></span></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nIn this text, we will explore how subtraction helps us measure whether two quantities are equal. Therefore, we recommend studying the previous texts in this chapter and the complete previous chapter (<htcite0>) before proceeding.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b5efc8f9-c268-430f-9681-7a43848ee48e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "First steps (Volume 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nIn the first text of the *First Steps* section, we began studying counting by arguing that our sides are equal (<htcite0>).\n\nIn the second chapter of the same section, we revisited the idea of equality by analyzing different objects and concluded that objects can be similar, but not entirely equal (<htcite1>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "20b14681-88c0-438a-a273-a0902ae5db38",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Our sides are equal",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2023",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b5efc8f9-c268-430f-9681-7a43848ee48e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "First steps (Volume 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Comparing</b><br /><br />Considering what we have seen so far, we can say that:<br />Addition helps us to group things together.<br />Multiplication helps us to group equal quantities.<br />Division helps us to share.<br />Subtraction helps us move quantities and show change.<br/><br/>If you had to choose one of these mathematical operations to determine if something is equal, which one would you choose?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_body",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Our Body\n\nTo answer the reflection's question, we will use our hands as represented in **Figure 1**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"book_navigation\"><tr><td>Left hand</td><td>Right hand</td></tr><tr><td><img id=\"imgLH\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgLH', '0%')\"  class=\"imgcenter\"/></td><td><img id=\"imgRH\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgRH', '0%')\"  class=\"imgcenter\"/></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><b>Figure 1</b>: Representation of the hands. © History Tracers. <span id=\"htImgCopyright\"></span></td></tr></table></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "The figure shows a person's two hands raised with all fingers extended, each pointing toward a fruit.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### What do I want to measure?\n\nTo test whether our hands are equal or equivalent (<htcite0>), we need to define what we will compare. For example, we can list three possible measurements:\n\n- Number of fingers;\n- Size of fingers;\n- Size of the hand.\n\nOnce we decide what to compare, we can state the result we expect. When we say our two hands are equal, we mean that no differences remain between them — the difference is zero for each corresponding measurement (<htcite1>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0200b385-9401-4d35-a4ae-66ed4826ea9c",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Igual",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "576e4648-77fa-4a6d-9e51-9459ced964f3",
                     "text": "Musa, Auwalu",
                     "page": "pp. 46 - 48",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_math",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Measuring the Difference\n\nLet's start with an exercise using our fingers. In **Figure 1**, both the left hand (L) and the right hand (R) have 5 fingers. We will perform operations with these numbers to see how we can state that the difference between the two hands is zero.\n\n#### Addition\n\nAddition was the first operation we learned. With it, we saw that we can build up quantities from zero, increasing by whatever we add (<htcite0>). So when we add 5 to the fingers we already have, the quantity grows, as shown in **Calculation 1**:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b5efc8f9-c268-430f-9681-7a43848ee48e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "First steps (Volume 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>10</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>10</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: Adding a constant to the number of fingers.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Multiplication\n\nMultiplication, which we learned is repeated addition (<htcite0>), would give us zero if we multiplied the number of fingers by zero (<htcite1>). That fact leads to a reflection:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "eaa59295-fb80-4c33-b1e4-bcdf3c11dabf",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "5",
                        "day": "9"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b6428e19-afcf-4d3b-916d-46e5a332ef53",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Understanding the Multiplication Table (0 and 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: Different path, same result</b><br /><br />If we multiply the number of fingers by zero, we get zero. But our goal is not to add zero five times (5 × 0); what we want is to measure whether two non-zero quantities are equal.<br /><br />Before this example, had you ever noticed that a calculation can be arithmetically correct and still fail to answer the question we asked?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Multiplying by zero does not help here, because we are comparing real, non-zero quantities (our own hands). And multiplying our fingers by any other number simply produces a new quantity: it never tells us how far apart two quantities are. For example, when we multiply our fingers by 5, as in **Calculation 2**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mn>5</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>25</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>25</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 2</b>: Multiplication of our fingers by a constant.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Division\n\nWhen we reached *Chapter 6* of the *First Steps* section, we encountered a new mathematical operation that helps us distribute what was accumulated: division (<htcite0>).\n\nAmong the operations seen so far, it is our best candidate for comparison, because it can decrease values instead of increasing them. Even so, with what we know so far, the best we could reach is **Calculation 3**:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b5efc8f9-c268-430f-9681-7a43848ee48e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "First steps (Volume 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 3</b>: Dividing the number of fingers by itself, as in Figure 1.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Before we see the next operation, notice that dividing zero by five (0/5) gives the same result as multiplying zero by five. Zero may be a correct answer, but it is not the comparison we want: we are not distributing anything among real hands; we are trying to compare those hands. Also notice that two equal quantities always divide to 1 — and 1 never signals the absence of a difference the way we need.\n\n#### Subtraction\n\nFinally, we arrive at the operation we are studying: subtraction.\n\nWe know this operation as one that puts quantities in motion, because matter cannot be eliminated in the universe — it is only transformed (<htcite0>).\n\nSo when we compare magnitudes, we move the differences until none remain. In other words, subtraction measures how far apart two quantities are: when the quantities are equal, the result is zero.\n\nWe can therefore compare the fingers of the two hands using subtraction as follows:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1dbe96e9-c7fa-4683-bdda-731e3e3f91ef",
                     "text": "Sterner, R. W., Small, G. E. & Hood, J. M.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2011",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">-</mo><mn>5</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">-</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 4</b>: Subtracting the fingers of one hand from the fingers of the other.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "With subtraction, we finally have the tool we wanted for comparison: an operation that measures the distance between two quantities.\n\nAfter comparing the number of fingers, we should repeat the same measurements with the size of each finger and of the hand as a whole. For those measurements we will use a ruler, as we learned in **When size matters** (<htcite0>); with it, we propose the following exercise.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "87f5f133-067e-439f-ab1d-eaf45e304079",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "When size matters",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 1: Are they equal?</b><br /><br />1. Take a sheet to note the results and a ruler to measure your fingers and hands.<br /><br />2. Take the measurements, noting the values for the left hand on the left side, and the values for the right hand on the right side.<br /><br />3. Subtract the value of one hand from the other; then subtract the pinky of the left hand from the pinky of the right; the ring finger of one hand from the ring finger of the other; and so on. Always compare corresponding fingers.</div></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Thanks to the symmetry of our body, most of these subtractions will be zero (or very close to it). Small differences may appear in your measurements — that is normal: no two hands are perfectly identical, and subtraction is exactly the tool that reveals those differences.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_generalization",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### General Comparison\n\nNow that we know subtraction is the operation that measures differences — and that the result is zero when nothing differs — we can write the comparison in general form. The difference between the left hand (L) and the right hand (R) is zero. As always, we respect the conservation of matter: the same operation is applied to both sides of the equality (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "87f5f133-067e-439f-ab1d-eaf45e304079",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "When size matters",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>L</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>R</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>L</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">-</mo><mn>R</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>R</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">-</mo><mn>R</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>L</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">-</mo><mn>R</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 5</b>: Comparison between two hands.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Each line of **Calculation 5** is actually a sentence describing exactly what we have done:\n\n- <b>Line 1:</b> The left hand is equal to the right hand.\n- <b>Line 2:</b> We subtracted the right hand (R) from both sides of the equality: the left hand minus the right equals the right hand minus itself.\n- <b>Line 3:</b> The difference between the left hand (L) and the right hand (R) does not exist — it is zero — which indicates that the hands are equal.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this text, we recalled the four mathematical operations we studied and concluded that subtraction is the best one for comparing quantities. The farther apart two magnitudes are, the larger the distance between them: that distance is the absolute difference between the quantities, obtained by subtracting the smaller value from the larger one — it is the path between them suggested by Graph 1. When the magnitudes are equal, the result is zero. In other words, measuring whether something is equal means observing the distance between the quantities being compared.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg width=\"420\" height=\"100\"  fill=\"none\" stroke=\"black\"> <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <line x1=\"2\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"15\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <text x=\"0\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">0</text> <text x=\"40\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">1</text> <text x=\"80\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">2</text> <text x=\"120\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">3</text> <text x=\"160\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">4</text> <text x=\"200\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">5</text> <text x=\"240\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">6</text> <text x=\"280\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">7</text> <text x=\"320\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">8</text> <text x=\"360\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"1.5em\">9</text> <line x1=\"5\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"5\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"45\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"45\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"125\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"125\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"165\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"205\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"205\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"245\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"245\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"285\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"285\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"325\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"325\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"365\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"365\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" />  </svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 1</b>: A path along the number line: each step is one unit of difference.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Finally, we learned that each step of a calculation is a complete description of what we are doing, written in a condensed form.",
               "source": null,
